Ladybird decides a permission request in the UI process, from the
document it knows made it, and shows one prompt for identical pending
requests. Chrome does the same in its browser process: Its
PermissionRequestManager answers a request that duplicates a pending
one along with that one (PermissionRequest::IsDuplicateOf()). Neither
stores anything for a prompt the user dismisses or ignores; both only
count it toward the embargo. Gecko and WebKit, too, decide these
prompts in their parent and UI processes rather than in the renderer.
